SELECT 'artist_biography', 'en', 1, '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Xiomara Fortuna — born in Monte Cristi in 1959 and known as the Queen of Fusion — is a Dominican singer and songwriter. Working from the island’s Afro-Dominican music — mangulina, salve, congos, gagá — and joining it to jazz and other contemporary forms, she has built a discography regarded as a valuable record of Afro-Dominican song, and in 2021 Rolling Stone ranked her album «Viendoaver» ninth among the year’s best Spanish-language records."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Monte Cristi","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Her mother introduced her to mangulina and to the work songs of the countryside. She came into the arts at twelve through theatre and local cultural groups, and as a teenager was drawn to the nueva trova and to songs with a social message, writing her first songs in those years. She sang in clubs and community centres in her town and across the Cibao before making her debut in the capital at a festival in the Teatro Nacional. She went on to study art at the Universidad Autónoma de Santo Domingo, but as a musician she is largely self-taught. She has named the «Movimiento 14 de Junio», the death of Trujillo, the war of 1965 and the history of slavery as the events that marked her music."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"«Kaliumbé»","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"With the guitarist Tony Vicioso she formed «Kaliumbé», the group in which she developed her style, fusing mangulina, priprí, salve, congos and gagá with jazz. Its first LP, «De la loma al llano» (1987), was financed by the women’s organisation «Mujeres en Desarrollo Dominicana». She also joined "},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"occurrenceId":"1c15ad68-2ac9-48d7-9cdd-5b4f2ad9b1e5","artistId":"20610d35-0190-4733-90c0-8c7db5cbe7e0","displayText":"Vickiana"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"occurrenceId":"3b1b92f0-b764-4c7e-89e3-e553a6b9fe2d","artistId":"2cc97ca9-126d-48c5-922f-e9d5c8b0360d","displayText":"Sonia Silvestre"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"occurrenceId":"77d6b414-4bfc-4f62-9797-1b1bd36470f0","artistId":"bc2289d0-ae94-48b5-8eb9-7f0ae18b845a","displayText":"Miriam Cruz"}},{"type":"text","text":", among others, on «Canto paz y un mundo nuevo»."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Europe and Africa","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In the 1990s her career moved to Europe, with performances in Switzerland and France; she worked with the saxophonist Archie Shepp and shared stages with Miriam Makeba and Touré Kunda, and also played in Russia and Cuba. «Balbuceos» (1996) and «Kumbajei» (1999) come from those years. Leading «Kaliumbé» and later «SinHora», she has taken her music to festivals in Nigeria, Ghana, Togo, Paris, Madrid, Moscow, Venezuela, Colombia, Port-au-Prince and Puerto Rico. Abroad her work was filed as Caribbean ethnic jazz; she prefers to call it Caribbean fusion."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A new generation","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In the new century she opened her sound to rock and song and to younger Dominican musicians. «Tonada para un querer» (2004) was followed by «Paseando» (2010), with guests including "},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"occurrenceId":"30af1abe-8eea-4723-9fa7-366ef3d9ec43","artistId":"34b63c95-f79a-4f7b-aa1d-426926d12959","displayText":"Pavel Núñez"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"occurrenceId":"30227a4e-bb5b-4a93-970f-b11a70f643ee","artistId":"e8f871cc-1119-4fab-a734-21a074e8df66","displayText":"Rita Indiana"}},{"type":"text","text":"; on «Pa cantarte a ti» and «Solo poemas» she